{
  "dom_id": "step-q352355",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)!}{x}^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{\u2032}(x).$\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\,\u22ef$",
  "worked_solution": "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)!}{x}^{n+1}=2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{\u2032}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=2-2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\u22ef.\n$$",
